1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,516
Now Who Picks Up the Phone?

2
00:00:03,516 --> 00:00:34,136
On March 26 the Israeli military said Revolutionary Guards navy commander Alireza Tangsiri had been killed in an airstrike in Bandar Abbas, and the Revolutionary Guards confirmed it. He had led the navy since 2018 and directed the blockade of Hormuz. The same day two teenagers were killed in strikes on homes in Shiraz, and in Abu Dhabi two civilians were killed by debris from an intercepted Iranian missile. The gatekeeper of the strait had been erased.

3
00:00:34,736 --> 00:01:08,932
One line on the status board changed. Lieutenant Kim Junho had been watching that line for twenty-seven days. The Revolutionary Guards navy commander, Tangsiri. The man who sorted the ships that could pass from the ships that would be hit. This morning the box next to his name said KIA presumed. He thought of the Vincennes on this sea in 1988. The one who is frightened shoots first. Henderson gave a short whistle. “The man holding the key ring is gone. Isn’t that good news for your ships?”

4
00:01:09,532 --> 00:01:36,624
“I don’t know, sir. He was the man who picked which ships got through. When somebody called, he was the one who picked up. Now who picks up that phone?” One captain had five days of water left. The part he needed was in Dubai, a hundred and twenty nautical miles away. It is easy to believe that if you kill the gatekeeper the gate will open. But the only one who knew where the key was hanging was the gatekeeper.

5
00:01:37,224 --> 00:01:41,036
Epic Fury continues in Episode 11.
